#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064125 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064281 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "73216188-6524-55c3-b945-86d13ef64170" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Trigonometric Function Using the Chain Rule"}] :question/id #uuid "2bb17470-6365-5e08-b649-f9f42146a4c6" :question/math-academy-id "e-732" :question/problem "Calculate $f'(x)$ if $f(x) = 2\\sin (3x - 1).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we express $f(x)$ as a composite function\n\n$$\nf = 2\\sin u, \\qquad u=3x-1.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d f}{\\textrm d u} = 2\\cos u, \\qquad\\dfrac{\\textrm d u}{\\textrm d x} = 3.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =6\\operatorname{cos}⁡u \\\\  & =6\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064126 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064282 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8ef81a0d-5a3a-5d7c-83e9-7d9ed0e8c5c6" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Reciprocal Trigonometric Function Using the Chain Rule"}] :question/id #uuid "7f56b401-c800-5708-8978-b84ce3f542f5" :question/math-academy-id "e-733" :question/problem "Calculate $\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x}$ if $y = 4\\csc (5x + 3).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we express $y$ as the composite function\n\n$$\ny = 4 \\csc u, \\qquad u = 5x+3.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d u} = -4\\csc u\\cot u, \\qquad\\dfrac{\\textrm d u}{\\textrm d x} = 5.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =-4\\operatorname{csc}⁡u\\operatorname{cot}⁡u\\cdot 5 \\\\  & =-20\\operatorname{csc}⁡u\\operatorname{cot}⁡u \\\\  & =-20\\operatorname{csc}⁡(5x+3)\\operatorname{cot}⁡(5x+3).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064127 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064283 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3ec11481-cea2-514b-aa78-707fcf8838cc" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Power of a Trigonometric Function Using the Chain Rule"}] :question/id #uuid "373a63fc-3dae-5880-8a49-63b5c64888f3" :question/math-academy-id "e-734" :question/problem "Given that $f(x) = \\sin^3{x}$, calculate $f'(x).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we write\n\n$$\nf(x) = (\\sin{x})^3.\n$$\n\nNow, we see that $f(x)$ can be expressed as the composite function\n\n$$\nf = u^3, \\qquad u=\\sin x.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d f}{\\textrm d u} = 3u^2, \\qquad \\dfrac{\\textrm d u}{\\textrm d x} = \\cos{x}.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =3{u}^{2}\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}⁡x) \\\\  & =3(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\  & =3{sin}^{2}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064129 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064284 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a483e892-90f2-51f4-80cd-2b1200cd0c37" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Composition of Trigonometric Functions Using the Chain Rule"}] :question/id #uuid "4ebd0289-b278-5352-b7bd-10ae218c43fb" :question/math-academy-id "e-735" :question/problem "Find the derivative of $y = \\tan{\\left(\\sin(x)\\right)}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we express $y$ as the composite function\n\n$$\ny = \\tan u, \\qquad u = \\sin x.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d u} = \\sec^2 u,\\qquad \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}u}{\\textrm d x} = \\cos x.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={sec}^{2}⁡u\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\  & ={sec}^{2}⁡(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233777 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064281 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "73216188-6524-55c3-b945-86d13ef64170" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Trigonometric Function Using the Chain Rule"}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "97de5fad-6e5e-571a-91b7-dfa67983b9d4" :question/math-academy-id "q-31241" :question/problem "Find the slope of $y=\\operatorname{sin}({x}^{2}+2x-1)$ at $x=0.$" :question/worked-solution "The slope of $y=f(x)$ at $x=0$ is given by ${f}^{'}(0).$\n\nWe will calculate ${f}^{'}(x)$ using the chain rule. First, we express $f(x)$ as the composite function\n\n$$\ny=\\operatorname{sin}u,\\,u={x}^{2}+2x-1.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}=\\operatorname{cos}u,\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=2x+2.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{'}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}u\\cdot (2x+2) \\\\  & =(2x+2)\\operatorname{cos}u \\\\  & =(2x+2)\\operatorname{cos}({x}^{2}+2x-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $x=0,$ we find\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{'}(0) & =(2\\cdot 0+2)\\operatorname{cos}({0}^{2}+2\\cdot 0-1) \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{cos}(-1) \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{cos}1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of $y=f(x)$ at $x=0$ is $2\\operatorname{cos}1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317267 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064284 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a483e892-90f2-51f4-80cd-2b1200cd0c37" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Composition of Trigonometric Functions Using the Chain Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fd80f4fc-d66d-5918-905b-16bb0d321fbc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{csc}x\\operatorname{cot}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316535}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fd80f4fc-d66d-5918-905b-16bb0d321fbc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{csc}x\\operatorname{cot}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316535} :answer-field/id #uuid "28742ecc-1707-5ebf-af66-6910320113a0" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316640} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cb68a3a2-59aa-56c5-bc1b-c4aa4acceb78" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{csc}(x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316268}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cb68a3a2-59aa-56c5-bc1b-c4aa4acceb78" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{csc}(x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316268} :answer-field/id #uuid "95746905-c009-54be-9389-495c9c01a41a" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316888}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "36a01249-dd25-5201-9ce2-0ee325c40559" :question/math-academy-id "q-342445" :question/problem "Given that $y=\\operatorname{sec}⁡(\\operatorname{csc}⁡(x)),$ we have\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=$\n{{field-1}}$\\cdot \\,\\operatorname{sec}⁡(\\operatorname{csc}⁡(x))\\cdot \\operatorname{tan}⁡($\n{{field-2}} $).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we express $y$ as the composite function\n\n$$\ny=\\operatorname{sec}⁡(u),\\,u=\\operatorname{csc}⁡(x).\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}=\\operatorname{sec}⁡(u)\\operatorname{tan}⁡(u),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-\\operatorname{csc}⁡x\\operatorname{cot}⁡x.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sec}⁡(u)\\operatorname{tan}⁡(u)\\cdot (-\\operatorname{csc}⁡x\\operatorname{cot}⁡x) \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{csc}⁡x\\operatorname{cot}⁡x\\cdot \\operatorname{sec}⁡(\\operatorname{csc}⁡(x))\\cdot \\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\operatorname{csc}⁡(x)).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329889 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064282 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8ef81a0d-5a3a-5d7c-83e9-7d9ed0e8c5c6" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Reciprocal Trigonometric Function Using the Chain Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "45e2c946-cebd-5416-8a76-6327b5030505" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327546} {:answer/id #uuid "8c9341d4-99b6-50f0-aa6d-9c6dd9382020" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328140} {:answer/id #uuid "acbeb3ba-53eb-5b9a-b551-835bc36de883" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=x-\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328432} {:answer/id #uuid "ca6bee3f-bd8c-5dcf-8359-5ca512f57072" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=3x-\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328708} {:answer/id #uuid "db84f8eb-8423-54a1-b09a-ae141285ed5e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=3x-\\frac{3\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328851}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "db84f8eb-8423-54a1-b09a-ae141285ed5e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=3x-\\frac{3\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328851} :answer-field/id #uuid "ac2d96d1-6187-53ac-b42b-8e94f57a3b62" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329484}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "33385857-91b0-513f-9a43-e506cd0cb163" :question/math-academy-id "q-48512" :question/problem "Find the equation of the tangent to the curve $y=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(2\\pi -3x)$ at the point $(\\frac{\\pi }{2},0).$" :question/worked-solution "First, the slope of $y=f(x)$ at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ is given by ${f}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2}).$\n\nWe will calculate ${f}^{′}(x)$ using the chain rule. First, we express $f(x)$ as the composite function\n\n$$\nf=\\operatorname{cot}⁡u,\\,u=2\\pi -3x.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}=-{csc}^{2}⁡u\\,\\text{and}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-3.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =(-{csc}^{2}⁡u)\\cdot (-3) \\\\  & =3{csc}^{2}⁡(2\\pi -3x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ we find\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m={f}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =3{csc}^{2}⁡(2\\pi -\\frac{3\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =3{csc}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =3\\cdot {1}^{2} \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we find the equation of the tangent at the point $(\\frac{\\pi }{2},0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-0 & =3(x-\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\ y & =3x-\\frac{3\\pi }{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064833 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064284 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a483e892-90f2-51f4-80cd-2b1200cd0c37" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Composition of Trigonometric Functions Using the Chain Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2b27ffe2-7676-50cf-8f85-63acc6816398" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327316} {:answer/id #uuid "446323eb-ee99-55ba-b53a-c33b7f83bceb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327531} {:answer/id #uuid "6e31cdb3-4a26-586a-a124-b350d2c5b39e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327889} {:answer/id #uuid "a5306166-8267-56b7-84f3-d958abd6020b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328359} {:answer/id #uuid "ad3ebbc8-ea7e-59c1-b950-2b9dc34de1a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328438}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "446323eb-ee99-55ba-b53a-c33b7f83bceb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327531} :answer-field/id #uuid "c5003d8b-4b77-55dc-a36e-b7375c9d128f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329529}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "55ba3ddf-3b81-51ce-a46b-d6facbbbea67" :question/math-academy-id "q-48643" :question/problem "Find the derivative of $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we express $y$ as the composite function\n\n$$\ny=\\operatorname{sin}⁡u,\\,u=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡u,\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x) \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064948 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064283 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3ec11481-cea2-514b-aa78-707fcf8838cc" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Power of a Trigonometric Function Using the Chain Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "18c16993-bae3-52c0-92ad-275cbeb50cdf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-9\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336262} {:answer/id #uuid "2e93c5d7-0745-5a8c-a9b3-de22c4366a79" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336481} {:answer/id #uuid "61070e19-b4f6-5780-b52f-32136383a579" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-9{cos}^{2}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336909} {:answer/id #uuid "97d86c60-9434-5c84-85a6-a6f88a1b92d0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18\\operatorname{sin}⁡x{cos}^{2}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337417} {:answer/id #uuid "a8a113ac-1f7d-54a3-aa47-90b10288ae2c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18\\operatorname{sin}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337557}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2e93c5d7-0745-5a8c-a9b3-de22c4366a79" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336481} :answer-field/id #uuid "2a78cc6f-1a98-5e11-9c07-21ff7e2374f2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338355}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "270b54b6-fdc6-5a75-af7c-f8052eee38ff" :question/math-academy-id "q-72154" :question/problem "Find ${f}^{′}(x)$ when $f(x)=9{sin}^{2}⁡(x).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we express\n\n$$\nf(x)=9(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)^{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, we see that $f(x)$ can be expressed as the composite function\n\n$$\nf=9{u}^{2},\\,u=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}=18u\\,\\text{and}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =18u\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\  & =18\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234318 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064284 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a483e892-90f2-51f4-80cd-2b1200cd0c37" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Composition of Trigonometric Functions Using the Chain Rule"}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d3114b1b-5508-592e-9c4c-63d9fe2d48cc" :question/math-academy-id "q-72163" :question/problem "Find the derivative of $y=\\operatorname{sin}(\\operatorname{sec}x).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we express $y$ as the composite function\n\n$$\ny=\\operatorname{sin}u,\\,u=\\operatorname{sec}x.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}=\\operatorname{cos}u,\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{sec}x\\operatorname{tan}x.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}u\\cdot (\\operatorname{sec}x\\operatorname{tan}x) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sec}x\\operatorname{tan}x\\operatorname{cos}(\\operatorname{sec}x).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
